{
  "meta": {
    "name": "Credit Counterfeit Index",
    "abbrev": "CCI",
    "definition": "CCI = Monetary Bind (mean z of HY OAS, SLOOS C&I tightening, real fed funds, inverted real M2 growth) minus Real Bind (genuine physical scarcity intensity). Credit is the counterfeiter when CCI >= threshold: tight money manufacturing scarcity that is not physically real.",
    "threshold": 1,
    "as_of": "2026-09-12",
    "sources": "Fed H.6 (M2, seasonally adjusted); Fed H.15 (effective federal funds rate); Fed Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey, July 2026 (the October survey is not yet published); BEA core PCE, July 2026 (3.3 percent year over year); ICE BofA US High Yield OAS (2.65 on 3 September). Real Bind is a published judgment series (Strait of Hormuz transit conditions, Brent, EIA Short-Term Energy Outlook). FRED is not reachable from the build environment; series are sourced from the issuing agencies directly.",
    "projection_note": "Entries with \"projected\": true are scenario estimates under the hawkish path, not observations.",
    "revision_note": "2026-09-12 reevaluation, and it reverses the direction of the August reading. The August entry set the Real Bind at 1.0 on the view that the acute phase had ended: the Strait reopened under the 18 June memorandum and the EIA expected flows back near pre-conflict levels by year end. That normalization did not hold. Transit has been selective rather than open, with Iran approving passage for ships that negotiated terms or paid a toll, and September brought a sharp escalation. Brent is averaging about $104.7 this month against $91.1 last month, and traded near $104.6 on 11 September. The Real Bind is therefore revised up to 1.15 for 2026Q3, not down. The monetary inputs barely moved: high yield spreads at 2.70, the SLOOS unchanged at 3, the real funds rate at 0.33 and real M2 growth at 2.1, giving a Monetary Bind of -0.09. The index reads -1.24 for 2026Q3, against the -1.08 the August entry carried for the same quarter. It is still rising quarter over quarter, from -1.36 in Q2, but by 0.12 rather than the 0.28 August reported, and the revision is downward. The point that survives from August is the mechanism: every move in this series is coming from the Real Bind, in whichever direction it happens to go, while the monetary side sits near zero and does almost nothing. The Q4 projection carries a rise to 3.75-4.00 percent at the 16 September meeting, largely offset in real terms by firmer energy prices, and a Real Bind of 1.20 that assumes the Oman track takes some heat out without restoring open transit. That projection is unusually wide on both sides and should be read as a scenario, not a forecast.",
    "known_limitation": "The Monetary Bind is built from aggregate inputs and cannot see the distribution beneath them. The July 2026 SLOOS is the clearest illustration: banks eased loan rate spreads for large and middle-market firms while a net share tightened credit card standards, and the aggregate C&I reading was 'basically unchanged'. A distributional companion is proposed but not yet scored, because no consistent history exists back to 2006. A second limitation is now visible in the series itself: the Real Bind is a quarterly judgment about physical constraint, and a chokepoint can reverse inside a quarter faster than the series can honestly be rescored. The August reading treated a partial reopening as a trend and was wrong."
  },
